title: Wakeup Alarms
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex11_Alarm1P.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex12_Alarm2P.em.ts
  - .assets/img-101-06-01.png
  - .assets/img-101-06-02.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |

      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Wakeup Alarms}

      This tour details two similar programs &ndash; both of which use a portable {[cu]AlarmMgr} to schedule wakeup events.

      Looking first at their signal captures, each program winks `AppLed` five times before halting:

      {[bi,counter_1]}{[cu]Alarm1P} alternately awakens every 0.75 or 2.00 sec&shy;onds, winking `AppLed` for 100 msecs.

      {[bi,counter_2]}{[cu]Alarm2P} awakens on _aligned_ 1.50 second intervals, alternately winking `AppLed` for 5 or 100 msecs.



      In fact, {[cu]AlarmMgr} uses a _real-time clock_ to track elapsed time from HW reset with 0.25 seconds of precision.

  - cmds:
      - close 3
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 4
      - mark 1 B 11
      - mark 1 C 17
      - mark 1 D 35

    focus: [1, 1]
    acts: [$reveal, $build]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]Alarm1P% &ndash; Details}

      <div class="em-bmlist">

      {[bm,A]} The {[cu]Alarm1P} program uses the {[cu]AlarmMgr} in a man&shy;ner not unlike the {[cu]FiberMgr} &ndash; as an _object-factory_.

      {[bm,B]} {[cu]Alarm1P} first declares {[cn]alarm} &ndash; a private {[cs]$config} parameter with a value of type {[ct]AlarmMgr}.{[ct]Obj}.

      {[bm,C]} After assigning {[cn]blinkF}, we bind {[cn]alarm} to the new object returned by {[cu]AlarmMgr}.{[cf]create} [**`meta`**].

      {[bm,D]} {[cu]Alarm1P} calls {[cf]wakeup} on its {[cn]alarm} object at run-time &ndash; scheduling a future {[cn]blinkF}.{[cf]post} dispatch.

      </div>

      Indeed, the {[cu]AlarmMgr} implementation will itself {[ck]import} the {[cu]FiberMg} &ndash; a common **EMS** design pattern.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - mark 2 D 35
    focus: [2, 1]
    acts: [$reveal, $build]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]Alarm2P% &ndash; Details}

      Virtually identical to {[cu]Alarm1P}, the {[cu]Alarm2P} program instead calls {[cf]wakeupAligned} {[bm,D]} on its {[cn]alarm} object.

      {[cf]wakeup} [{[cu]Alarm1P}] versus {[cf]wakeupAligned} [{[cu]Alarm2P}] use their _dt_ arg differently in scheduing future wakeup _t_:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cf]wakeup}: `t = now + dt`<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cf]wakeupAligned}: `t = ceil(now / dt) * dt`

      In the preceding formulas, _now_ represents the current value of a real-time clock available to {[cu]AlarmMgr}.

      {[in]The %[cu]AppLed%.%[cf]wink% method seen here internally uses %[cu]Poller% &ndash; just as we did in the previous tour.}

  - cmds:
      - view 3
      - view 4
    text: |

      {[ht,monitor_heart]&thinsp;%[cu]Alarm2P% &ndash; Signal Capture}

      Zooming-in after 3000&thinsp;ms of execution time, {[cu]Alarm2P} awakens from MCU sleep to wink `AppLed` for 5&thinsp;ms.

      By using {[cf]wakeupAligned}, the `AppLed` winks will start precisely after &lbrace;1500, 3000, 4500, &hellip;&thinsp;&rbrace;&thinsp;ms from reset.

      {[cu]Alarm2P} in fact enters _two_ distinct modes of sleep:

      {[bm,D]} a _deep_ [&mu;J] sleep, for intervals measured in secs<br>
      {[bm,L]} a _lite_ [mJ] sleep, for intervals measured in msecs

      While _deep-sleep_ yields 10× savings in energy con&shy;sumption, _lite-sleep_ wakeups happen 10× faster.

      {[in]Finally, note how <code>DbgB</code> pulses <i>once</i> %[bm,L]% or <i>twice</i> %[bm,D]% when entering lite-sleep or deep-sleep mode.}

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Use **F12** to view the {[cf]Secs30p2_initMsecs} code.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Vary the durations (in msecs) used by {[cu]Alarm2P}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Switch to {[cf]wakeup} and study the signal capture.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      Imagine _two_ fibers inside the {[cu]Alarm2P} pro&shy;gram &ndash; each performing a different function at a different rate.

      To simplify this design, the next tour examines a port&shy;able solution building upon {[cu]AlarmMgr} and {[cu]FiberMgr}.

      {[hc]}
